# Prism backup groups: verification and manual space release

`charts/prism/files/prism-backup.sh` is the program mounted into the existing
Prism backup jobs. Completed `backup-*` directories use the existing
`prism-db-artifacts-v1` format: a PostgreSQL custom dump, immutable object files,
exact object index and checksummed metadata. Flat legacy dumps remain untouched.
No schedule deletes groups. An incomplete or interrupted operation consumes
capacity until an operator selects it for removal.

This is **not a complete disaster-recovery system**. Keys and cross-service
journals are not yet bound into these groups. The scheduled `database-proof` is
only a same-server SQL smoke test. Complete session/job/pipeline recovery,
independent failure-domain recovery and measured RPO/RTO remain unverified.

## Capacity and protection contract

The chart's `backup.maximumBytes`, `backup.maximumRetainedBytes` and
`backup.maximumDurationSeconds` bound a group, retained storage and execution.
Retained usage includes incomplete groups. Capacity exhaustion fails the backup;
it never evicts existing recovery points. Provision space for active capture,
retained recovery points and interrupted work. The script reserves additional
headroom, so the next group's payload alone is not the required free space.

The lexically latest completed group (the program generates UTC timestamp names)
is always protected. Retirement also verifies that latest group; a corrupted
latest group blocks release. Do not rename production groups. For every additional
point required by your recovery policy, create an empty marker named
`.protect-<complete-group-name>` in the backup root. Create/remove markers while
holding the same exclusive `<BACKUP_ROOT>/.lock`; marker creation without the lock
can race with retirement. Protection markers live outside immutable groups.
A marker's existence, including a symlink, prevents retirement.

There is no age-based expiry. An operator must decide which point is no longer
required locally and preserve an identical verified copy. This implements D07's
manual release rule, without deleting the only known copy of project material.
The operator must separately define required historical points, external storage,
key custody and recovery targets. A different directory or mount path does **not**
prove a different physical failure domain. Protect the copy and periodically test
its recovery in the destination environment.

## Retire one completed group

Run in a controlled maintenance environment with Bash, GNU coreutils/findutils
and util-linux `flock`, under the UID that owns backup files. Mount the existing
backup root and the verified-copy root writable: both require their own `.lock`.
Do not run uncoordinated backup-file edits or deletion alongside this program.
Every cooperating command serializes through those locks.

Set the normal program configuration to the actual chart limits, then select an
exact group and its **previously recorded SHA-256 of `SHA256SUMS`**. Do not derive
the expected digest from an untrusted copy at the moment of retirement.

```sh
export BACKUP_ROOT=/backups
export BACKUP_MAXIMUM_BYTES=1073741824
export BACKUP_MAXIMUM_RETAINED_BYTES=8589934592
export BACKUP_MAXIMUM_DURATION_SECONDS=3600
export BACKUP_RETIRE_GROUP=backup-20260920T120000Z-REPLACE
export BACKUP_COPY_ROOT=/verified-copies
export BACKUP_RETIRE_SHA256=REPLACE_WITH_RECORDED_64_HEX_DIGEST
bash /backup-program/prism-backup.sh retire
```

Values above are examples, not deployment defaults. The copy must be an intact
same-named group at `<BACKUP_COPY_ROOT>/<BACKUP_RETIRE_GROUP>`. Root overlap,
wrong digest, corrupt or missing objects, an active copy-root lock, protection,
or selection of the latest group aborts without releasing that group. Both
manifests must match and both complete groups are verified before the first
rename. Symlinked roots/groups are rejected. Hash pinning detects mismatch; it
is not a digital signature or a defense against a fully compromised operator.

Release atomically renames the selected directory to `.retiring-<group-name>`,
syncs the backup filesystem, then removes only that directory. A killed process
may leave a partially removed tombstone. Repeat the **same** retirement command
with the same pinned copy to finish. The copy and remaining latest local group
are reverified before continuing. Never treat a `.retiring-*` directory as a
recovery point. A successful command prints `PRISM_BACKUP_RETIRED:<group-name>`.

## Discard one failed capture

First inspect the failure and confirm that its private incomplete directory is
no longer needed for diagnosis. Select the exact `.incomplete-*` name:

```sh
export BACKUP_DISCARD_INCOMPLETE=.incomplete-20260920T120000Z-REPLACE
bash /backup-program/prism-backup.sh discard-incomplete
```

The same root lock prevents concurrent capture from being removed. The command
rejects completed names, traversal and symlinked directories. Incomplete work has
never been published as a restore point and therefore does not require a copied
recovery group. No wildcard or age-based purge is supported. An interrupted
cleanup can be repeated while the directory exists; after successful removal,
repeating it reports a missing directory. Existing complete groups and legacy
flat dumps are untouched.

## Local evidence and remaining work

`prism-backup.test.mts` exercises filesystem copying, verification, retention,
protection, capacity, SIGKILL after retirement rename, partial tombstone resume,
invalid names, source corruption, locks, timeouts and chart rendering. Its DB
commands are explicitly fixtures and prove no PostgreSQL behavior.

`prism-backup-native.test.mts` requires two real PostgreSQL servers with different
system identifiers and data directories. It executes the original migrations and
Prism revision/artifact APIs, creates repeated groups, reaches quota, releases one
group, captures again and restores that new dump on the other server. It compares
all Prism table rows from a quiescent snapshot, reads copied objects after removal
of the source object directory and edits the restored document through the real
repository. It does not exercise saved jobs/sessions or concurrent writers; it
is a Q4 storage roundtrip and partial Q3 evidence, not a full Q3 acceptance test.

The native gate fails when required servers are absent; it never silently skips.
Both URLs must point at dedicated disposable loopback servers. The test owns only
randomly named databases it creates, but original migrations create the normal
Prism roles and vector extension, so do not supply shared developer databases.

```sh
PRISM_BACKUP_TEST_SOURCE_URL=postgresql://USER@127.0.0.1:55432/postgres \
PRISM_BACKUP_TEST_TARGET_URL=postgresql://USER@127.0.0.1:55433/postgres \
node --experimental-transform-types --test tests/verification/deployment/prism-backup-native.test.mts
```
